You are a senior full-stack engineer and product-minded architect. Build a production-ready MVP for a web app called **note.dev**.

The product:
note.dev is a learning-focused programming notebook platform. It combines structured notes, reusable folders/notebooks, runnable code snippets, and simple manual diagram templates in one place. It is not a generic note-taking app and not a full Notion clone. The core idea is that a learner should be able to write concepts, insert code examples, run them, add simple hand-drawn-style templates for data structures or algorithm ideas, save everything to their account, and revisit it later.

Important product principles:
- Notes should feel like a programmer’s notebook, not a document editor.
- Output from code execution should be temporary and should not be permanently saved as part of the note.
- Users should be able to organize knowledge into notebooks/folders and notes.
- Diagram blocks should be simple premade templates users can fill in manually. Do not build heavy automatic algorithm visualization.
- The app should support learning workflows for DSA, web development, and language practice.

Tech stack constraints:
- Frontend: JavaScript, HTML, CSS, Bootstrap
- Do NOT use TypeScript
- Do NOT use Tailwind
- Backend: Node.js + Express
- Database/Auth/Storage: Supabase
- Keep dependencies minimal
- Prefer clean, maintainable, readable code over clever abstractions
- The app should be built with vanilla JavaScript where practical, using Bootstrap and custom CSS for UI

Primary feature set to implement:

1. Authentication and user accounts
- Users can sign up, log in, log out
- Use Supabase Auth
- Every user gets a private workspace
- No user should be able to access another user’s notes or notebooks
- Include proper session handling
- Redirect unauthenticated users away from protected areas

2. Workspace model
Each user has a personal workspace containing:
- notebooks / folders
- notes inside notebooks
- note blocks inside notes
- code snippets inside notes
- diagram/template blocks inside notes

Users must be able to:
- create notebooks
- rename notebooks
- delete notebooks
- create notes inside notebooks
- rename notes
- delete notes
- move notes between notebooks
- search notes
- open a note and continue editing it later

3. Notes editor
The note editor should support:
- plain text
- headings
- bullet lists
- code blocks / snippets
- simple diagram/template blocks
- autosave

The note writing experience should feel lightweight and practical, not like a complex document editor. A simple, clean editor is enough if it is reliable and fast.

4. Code snippets
Notes can contain multiple code snippets.

Supported languages:
- JavaScript
- Python
- C++
- HTML
- CSS

Snippet behavior:
- Each snippet has its own Run button
- Each snippet has its own output panel
- Output should be temporary and not saved permanently with the note
- Re-running a snippet should replace the current output
- If the page reloads, output should be empty until the code is run again
- The code itself must be saved
- The result/output must not be stored as a persistent note field

Language behavior:
- JavaScript should run in-browser when possible
- HTML/CSS should support a preview mode so users can see how markup and styles render together
- Python and C++ should be handled through a backend execution flow if needed
- If full secure execution is too large for the first pass, structure the system so these languages can be added cleanly
- Keep sandboxing and safety in mind
- Do not expose the server to arbitrary unsafe execution without clear isolation

5. HTML/CSS preview mode
A key feature is a beginner-friendly preview for web development.
- Users should be able to write HTML and CSS and preview the result easily
- The preview should make it obvious how the code maps to the rendered page
- The feature should reduce confusion for beginners learning web development
- Keep it visually clear and simple

6. Diagram/template blocks
Instead of automatic algorithm visualization, provide manual templates that act like structured digital notebook sketches.

Include premade templates for:
- Stack
- Queue
- Linked List
- Tree
- Graph
- Array
- Sliding Window
- Two Pointers
- Heap

These should behave like editable visual blocks:
- simple boxes, lines, labels, and placeholders
- users can type inside them or modify them manually
- no animated algorithm engine required
- no heavy drag-and-drop dependency unless it stays simple
- the goal is to let learners build their own learning diagrams quickly

7. Storage and persistence
- Save all user work to Supabase
- Persist notebooks, notes, and snippet code
- Persist user structure and organization
- Do not persist code execution output as permanent content
- Autosave note changes
- Support reloading the app and restoring the workspace reliably

8. Search and navigation
- Add a sidebar navigation layout
- Show notebooks/folders in the sidebar
- Show notes within a selected notebook
- Add search by note title and note content
- Make switching between notes fast
- Keep navigation simple and intuitive

9. UI/UX requirements
The interface should be:
- clean
- modern
- learning-focused
- responsive on desktop and mobile
- easy to scan
- not cluttered

Use Bootstrap components for structure and responsiveness. Add custom CSS for polish where needed.

Add obvious actions such as:
- New notebook
- New note
- Add code block
- Add diagram block
- Run snippet
- Save status indicator

The UI should feel like a serious productivity tool, but still approachable.

10. Backend requirements
Build an Express backend to support:
- authentication integration if needed
- any server-side code execution endpoints
- note and notebook operations if you choose not to call Supabase directly from the browser
- data validation
- basic error handling
- secure API design

Organize the backend with clear routes, controllers, and services if helpful.

11. Database design
Design a clean schema using Supabase tables such as:
- users or profiles
- notebooks
- notes
- note_blocks
- snippets

Possible fields:
- id
- user_id
- notebook_id
- note_id
- title
- content
- language
- order_index
- created_at
- updated_at

Make sure the schema supports:
- multiple notebooks per user
- multiple notes per notebook
- multiple blocks per note
- multiple snippets per note if needed

Use row-level security so users can only access their own data.

12. Security and access control
- Enforce user data isolation
- Use Supabase row-level security policies
- Validate all inputs
- Prevent one user from reading or editing another user’s workspace
- Avoid unsafe execution patterns
- Treat code execution carefully

13. MVP scope
Build the best possible MVP first. The first version should focus on:
- auth
- notebooks/folders
- notes
- code snippets
- output panel
- HTML/CSS preview
- diagram templates
- autosave
- search
- private user workspace

Do not spend time on:
- collaboration
- shared workspaces
- public publishing
- comments
- likes
- social feeds
- AI assistant features
- mobile apps
- real-time multiplayer editing
- overly complex diagram engines
- advanced analytics

14. Code quality expectations
- Use a clean folder structure
- Write readable, modular JavaScript
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction
- Keep components and functions focused
- Use sensible naming
- Include comments only where they help clarity
- Include setup instructions
- Include environment variable examples
- Include database migration or schema instructions
- Include Supabase setup guidance
- Include a reasonable deployment plan

15. Delivery expectations
When implementing, proceed in logical phases:
- Phase 1: authentication + workspace structure
- Phase 2: notes editor + autosave
- Phase 3: code blocks + run flow
- Phase 4: HTML/CSS preview
- Phase 5: diagram/template blocks
- Phase 6: search + cleanup + polish

For each phase:
- make the code work end-to-end
- keep the app runnable
- avoid leaving broken placeholders
- prefer a working simpler solution over an incomplete ambitious one

Final product vision:
note.dev should feel like a focused learning notebook for developers. The user should be able to create a private account, organize notebooks, write notes, insert runnable code examples, preview HTML/CSS, sketch common data structures with templates, and return later to continue learning from the same place.

Build this as a serious MVP with room to grow, but do not overengineer it.